# `SUBJECT-VOCABULARY-SCOPE`/`SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`: subject vocabulary is PER GENRE, and `topology`
# declares exactly three subject words of its own — `group`, `external`,
# `bundle`. This fixture is the KEEP half of the grid for this genre: all
# three must still parse. The WITHDRAW half is 241.
#
# `group` and `external` keep their `block` spellings, and the ruling records
# why rather than leaving it to look like inheritance: every networking
# synonym is more taken — `zone` (DNS, firewall), `cluster` (RFC 4456),
# `domain` (RFC 7926), `area` (OSPF), `site` (EVPN) — and both collisions are
# SOFT, because the picture contradicts the wrong reading. A multicast group
# is never drawn as a box round its members, and an `external` is never drawn
# at all (`EXTERNAL-EDGE-ENDPOINTS`). Two genres agreeing on a spelling is two independent
# declarations that happen to agree, never one inherited (core §3, `GENRE-VOCABULARY-OBLIGATION`).
#
# `bundle` is the cell defined BY ITS REFERENT: a LAG (IEEE 802.1AX), an ECMP
# set, an EVPN Ethernet Segment. That is why it survives here and was
# withdrawn from `block`, which has no referent to name.
figdown 0.1 topology
group pod "Pod 1"
node leaf1 "Leaf-1" in=pod
node leaf2 "Leaf-2" in=pod
external wan "to WAN"
edge leaf1 -- wan
edge leaf2 -- wan
bundle es1 "ES-1" leaf1--wan,leaf2--wan
